# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What must NOT be uploaded to Vercel.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `.gitignore` governs git; it does NOT govern what the
# Vercel CLI uploads. Without this file the first deployment shipped `.env` —
# real source and application database passwords, and the Gemini key — into the
# deployment bundle, where they are outside secret management and cannot be
# rotated from the dashboard. Nothing in `.gitignore` prevented it, because
# nothing in `.gitignore` was being consulted.
#
# Environment variables belong in Project Settings -> Environment Variables.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Secrets. Never upload. -------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env

*.pem
*.key
credentials.json
secrets.json

# --- CST rule documents -----------------------------------------------------
# Both casings: the folder is `knowledge-source` on a case-insensitive Windows
# disk and matching is case-sensitive elsewhere, so one pattern is not enough.
#
# NOTE: excluding these means draft generation on Vercel has no rules and will
# state no policy. That is the SAFE default, not the finished answer — one of
# these workbooks ("B2B customers.xlsx") carries customer contact data and must
# never be deployed. Shipping the twelve rule workbooks without it, or loading
# them from storage, is a decision still to be made.
/Knowledge-source/
/knowledge-source/

# --- Not needed at runtime --------------------------------------------------
# Keeps the serverless bundle down; the build already warns that dynamic
# filesystem access causes the whole project to be traced into it.
/tests/
/scripts/
/migrations/
/.claude/

# --- Local artefacts --------------------------------------------------------
*.tsbuildinfo
/coverage/
/.next/
/node_modules/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
